Test data | Category  | Expected result           | Actual result
----------|-----------|----------------------------|----------------------------
65        | Normal    | Accepted, stored as 65     | Accepted, stored as 65
0         | Boundary  | Accepted, stored as 0      | Accepted, stored as 0
100       | Boundary  | Accepted, stored as 100    | Accepted, stored as 100
-1        | Erroneous | Rejected; error message    | Rejected; error message shown
101       | Erroneous | Rejected; error message    | Accepted, stored as 101  <-- FAULT

-- Reading the table --
// The -1 row shows the LOWER boundary is being enforced correctly: -1 is rejected,
//   exactly as expected, so no fault is revealed there.
// The 101 row shows a genuine fault: 101 is outside the valid range and should have
//   been rejected, but the actual result shows the program accepted it anyway --
//   most likely because the validation condition was coded with < 101 (or <= 101)
//   instead of the correct <= 100, an off-by-one mistake at exactly the kind of
//   boundary this erroneous test value was specifically chosen to reveal.
// Note that 101 itself is ERRONEOUS data here, not boundary data: the boundary
//   values for this rule are 0 and 100, the valid values at the very edges of the
//   range; 101 is the value just past the upper boundary, so it belongs outside
//   the valid range entirely, in the erroneous category.
